WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.966
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:09.745
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.045 --> 00:00:17.875
Unit fourteen: Technical Reports. Today you will specify processes and methods with preposition + which relative clauses.

4
00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:20.851
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.754
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:27.105
Executive summary.

7
00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:32.717
Executive summary means a short overview of a report for busy decision makers.

8
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:36.001
The executive summary appears on page one.

9
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:39.443
Your turn. Say: Executive summary.

10
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.858
Executive summary.

11
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:46.280
Methodology section.

12
00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:51.767
Methodology section means the part of a report that explains how the work was done.

13
00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:55.207
The methodology section lists each test.

14
00:00:55.817 --> 00:00:58.980
Your turn. Say: Methodology section.

15
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:03.438
Methodology section.

16
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:05.730
Data visualization.

17
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:10.864
Data visualization means turning numbers into charts, maps or diagrams.

18
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.053
Data visualization made the trend obvious.

19
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.790
Your turn. Say: Data visualization.

20
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.157
Data visualization.

21
00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:24.730
Technical specification.

22
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:29.992
Technical specification means a precise description of what a product must do.

23
00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:33.686
The technical specification sets the maximum weight.

24
00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:38.108
Your turn. Say: Technical specification.

25
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.792
Technical specification.

26
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.760
Results section.

27
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.514
Results section means the part of a report that presents what was found.

28
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:52.650
The results section includes three tables.

29
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.123
Your turn. Say: Results section.

30
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:00.630
Results section.

31
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.559
Appendix.

32
00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:07.163
Appendix means extra material placed at the end of a report.

33
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.005
The raw data appears in the appendix.

34
00:02:10.617 --> 00:02:13.408
Your turn. Say: Appendix.

35
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.337
Appendix.

36
00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:19.781
Audience adaptation.

37
00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:25.128
Audience adaptation means changing the style and detail to suit the readers.

38
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:28.517
Audience adaptation helps non-experts follow.

39
00:02:29.117 --> 00:02:32.164
Your turn. Say: Audience adaptation.

40
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.616
Audience adaptation.

41
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.839
Clarity and precision.

42
00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:43.858
Clarity and precision means writing that is easy to understand and exact.

43
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.123
Clarity and precision prevent costly errors.

44
00:02:47.717 --> 00:02:51.217
Your turn. Say: Clarity and precision.

45
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.031
Clarity and precision.

46
00:02:56.833 --> 00:03:00.763
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.617 --> 00:03:08.065
Visal, a report says 'The method by which the samples were tested is described below.' Why 'by which'?

48
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:20.489
A preposition can come before which or whom in formal writing. The method by which… the process in which… the team with whom we worked. It sounds precise and impersonal.

49
00:03:21.583 --> 00:03:23.386
What is the informal version?

50
00:03:24.483 --> 00:03:36.670
The method which the samples were tested by. This is correct but less formal. With a preposition first, use which for things and whom for people, never that or who.

51
00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.853
Why use it in technical reports?

52
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:52.902
It specifies exactly how, where or under what conditions something happens: the conditions under which the device fails. Do not stack too many; it can make a sentence heavy.

53
00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:55.613
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:01.398
Grammar focus: Formal relative clauses: preposition plus which and whom.

55
00:04:01.698 --> 00:04:02.862
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.036
The method by which the samples were tested is described below.

57
00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.575
The conditions under which the device fails are listed in the appendix.

58
00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:15.435
The engineers with whom we worked approved the design.

59
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:25.786
In formal writing, put the preposition before which (things) or whom (people): the method by which, the engineers with whom. Never use that or who after a preposition.

60
00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.096
Be careful! Don't say: The method which by it works is new.

61
00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:34.799
Say: The method by which it works is new.

62
00:04:35.099 --> 00:04:37.168
Put the preposition before which.

63
00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:40.607
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.703
Writing with Precision.

65
00:04:43.003 --> 00:04:48.474
A technical report opens with an executive summary, a short overview for busy readers.

66
00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:53.486
The methodology section describes the process by which the data were gathered.

67
00:04:54.500 --> 00:05:00.017
The results section uses data visualization, through which complex numbers become clear patterns.

68
00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:06.284
The appendix contains the technical specification to which the report often refers.

69
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.854
Audience adaptation matters most when the readers are people for whom the subject is new.

70
00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:16.864
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.123
This is the process blank the parts are joined.

72
00:05:20.423 --> 00:05:24.030
A, by which. B, which by. C, what by.

73
00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:29.682
The answer is A: by which.

74
00:05:30.817 --> 00:05:34.233
Extra material placed at the end of a report is an blank.

75
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:38.592
A, appendix. B, abstract. C, executive.

76
00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.261
The answer is A: appendix.

77
00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:46.975
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:54.086
A, The team with whom we worked agreed.. B, The team with who we worked agreed.. C, The team who with we worked agreed..

79
00:05:57.633 --> 00:06:01.049
The answer is A: The team with whom we worked agreed..

80
00:06:02.183 --> 00:06:04.810
Turning numbers into charts is data blank.

81
00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:09.971
A, adaptation. B, visualization. C, specification.

82
00:06:13.533 --> 00:06:16.009
The answer is B: visualization.

83
00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:20.740
According to the reading, who needs audience adaptation most?

84
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.669
A, readers new to the subject. B, the printer. C, the author only.

85
00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:32.150
The answer is A: readers new to the subject.

86
00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.611
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:42.398
Why is clarity often more important than technical detail when writing for a general audience?

88
00:06:51.183 --> 00:06:52.707
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:53.007 --> 00:07:19.834
Clarity is often more important because general readers cannot use detail that they do not understand. If the report is too technical, the main message is lost and people may make poor decisions. A clear executive summary and good audience adaptation explain the key points first, and the exact technical specification can still be placed in the appendix for experts.

90
00:07:20.134 --> 00:07:21.843
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:29.721
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal relative clauses: preposition plus which and whom.

92
00:07:30.021 --> 00:07:33.893
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:07:34.193 --> 00:07:36.868
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.168 --> 00:07:39.937
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

